{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789426558,"nonce":"bfPo8HbuXhRDHeKO0ZOBn2KKflM=","title":"Wren threads and isolates on the core heap","message":"wren_lift's Thread (tasks on a worker pool, one shared heap) and Isolate (a VM per thread, its own heap) landed 2026-09-14 (6771870, f801d0e, with the VM/Shared split 249c239 and its own stop-the-world, stw.rs). Under caribou-wren neither works yet:\n\n- Thread: the heap record's lists (`pins`, `views`, `held`) are the VM thread's, pushed on every allocation with no lock; two workers allocating corrupts malloc (exit 133). The worker threads are also never registered with the core: the core's stop cannot bring them to rest and its scan does not see their stacks.\n- Isolate: an isolate's VM is made on its own thread through `heap_new`, so it registers; but the main thread parks in wren_lift's scheduler (`Sched::idle`, a condvar wait) which is no core safepoint, so an isolate's cycle, ending in the core's collection, waits 2 s for it and gives up; the program wedges. Also the isolate's VM comes from `parent.isolate_factory`, so the driver has to supply one that applies `import::configure` and enters the VM on the isolate's thread (`enter_vm`), else host calls from the isolate find no VM.\n\nWhat caribou can do alone: lists that several threads may push (a lock, or per-thread pins merged at the cycle), lazy registration of a thread on its first allocation (a thread-local guard that unregisters at exit), the isolate factory in the driver. What needs wren_lift's seam (filed there as f7bdd71): thread start/stop, the safe/running transitions stw.rs already makes (for the core to treat a safe thread as a blocking region and scan it where it stands), and a poll from the safepoint page's slow path so a compiled loop honours the core's stop. Until those land, a Wren program that uses Thread or Isolate runs on wren_lift's own heap only (`caribou-wren --no-install`).\n\nDesign note: the concurrency page says a Thread is a Fiber that runs on another OS thread; that is the same object the core's scheduler calls a task, and Ash's threads already are core tasks through Ash's seam. The end state is one task object both languages see as their own, the way the cell is one object both see as their own; then the host's and wren_lift's worlds are one world.","files":null}]}